To start our Flask application, first we will check if our virtual environment is active.
For that, we will open a terminal session and look for the name of our virtual environment within parentheses before the command prompt. We should see something like this:
(.venv) user@user FlaskProject %

If we don't see our virtual environment name, we will search in our project to see if it exists. If not, we will run this command to create one:
- for Linux / MacOS: python3 -m venv .venv (or virtualenv .venv)
- for Windows: python -m venv .venv

Then we can activate our virtual environment by running the following command:
- for Linux / MacOS: source .venv/bin/activate
- for Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate

After our venv is active, we will install the libraries that are necessary for running our Flask application.
pip install -r requirements.txt (or pip3 install -r requirements.txt)

What does this command do? It will take one by one each library from requirements.txt and it will install it.

The last step is to start our application. We will start our application by running our application file like any other Python script:
python <file_name> (or python3 <file_name>)

This will open a connection at the following address: http://127.0.0.1:5000.
From here we can access the routes that we created in our script.
